{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/390293-linguistic-psychology-bilingualism-help","id":390293,"title":"Linguistic Psychology & Bilingualism - help?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-02T13:31:54-08:00","id":390293,"title":"Linguistic Psychology & Bilingualism - help?","content":"This will sound random - but a lot of fellow-geeks on this forum - so I'm hoping one of you has some useful insights or links.<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> I'm natively bilingual (Spanish / English) - so even though I <u>intuitively</u> can feel how these languages are different (and how these differences can snowball into differences in thought and culture) - I'm struggling to find articles on the subject (mostly, I suspect, because people who learn additional languages as an academic exercise don't grasp it the way someone who acquires it as a native). It's one of those things where the nuances really stick out (but you need to notice them first).<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> <b>A Few Examples:</b><br /> <br /> Some academics noticed that aborigines in Australia speak a language with a very strong sense of orientation (North / South / East / West). Whereas in English, such directions are only used for large distances - they also use them for small distances (you might say &quot;your (East/South/North/West) foot&quot; as opposed to &quot;your (Left/Right) foot&quot;. The result is that even 5 year olds could tell how everyone was oriented (no windows, no clues) - even when grown scientists could not.<br /> <br /> Words often have multiple meanings - so the &quot;cloud of meaning&quot; around a word is very different between languages. For example, &quot;I care about you&quot; vs &quot;Te quiero&quot; --- in English you can &quot;care about someone&quot; (but not feel the same way), you &quot;take care&quot; of problems and children, you &quot;don't care&quot;, or are &quot;careless&quot;. Against &quot;quiero&quot; tho - the meaning is more of a cloud-combination of &quot;to want&quot; and &quot;to care for&quot;. You need to use an entirely different word to &quot;care&quot; for sick relatives. And where you'd say someone was &quot;careless&quot; (implying fault on their end for not being responsible), the closest translation in Spanish would be to do something &quot;sin quierer&quot; (implying it was not their fault; something entirely accidental or coincidental).<br /> <br /> This coincides with an article I saw on how English is usually very clear about blame (you're more likely to say &quot;John broke the glass&quot;) whereas Spanish and Japanese only do so when an action was deliberate (and so the closest equivalent we're <i>more likely to use </i> is akin to &quot;the glass broke&quot;). This isn't a difference in memory (English, Spanish and Japanese speakers all remembered - equally well - when someone <u>deliberately</u> broke something) -- it's a difference in language (such that Spanish and Japanese do not value holding blame for accidental things - so those speakers tended to forget who was responsible when they didn't mean to do it).<br /> <br /> This also extends to an <i>intuitive</i> grasp..... not only do we have more love/sex/cheat songs in Spanish, but the phrasing is much more ambiguous (and this is a <u>good</u> thing for us). I've heard songs that could be translated <u>equally</u> well as a lover asking &quot;how serious will you let me take this relationship with you?&quot; OR &quot;where can I meet you tonight?&quot; OR &quot;how deep can you handle me?&quot; OR even &quot;where can I cum?&quot;. <br /> The beauty (for us) is that all are equally viable and equally deniable ---- allowing your lover to hear what they're in the mood for --- allowing you to know where things stand and work from there. Thus, <i>in practice</i>, as a Latino dating Latinas (of whatever color), I've had much more of the &quot;fight all morning, fuck all evening&quot; experience. I've only ever encountered the sitcom-trope of &quot;cold shoulder&quot; or &quot;sleep on the couch&quot; when dating an Anglophone. <br /> <br /> Or vocabulary differences! <u>In common parlance</u>, our news might say &quot;Asesino&quot; (killer) or &quot;Ladr\u00f3n&quot; (rogue) -- but Americans make crime into an art form! I'd have to <i>explain</i> to my family if I wanted to describe &quot;un asesino&quot; such as the difference between a Killer, a Murderer, a Mass Murderer, a Serial Killer, a Hitman, an Assassin --- or a Burglar, a Mugger, a Pickpocket, a Con Artist, a Forger, etc. I imagine this makes sense because the United States colonies began as a mixture of religious fanaticism and criminals --- conversely, Spanish colonies forbade single-women from traveling to the New World (hence Spaniards very quickly interbred with African and Indigenous women, helping us form a new mixed identity). In turn, no matter what you <i>look</i> like, genetically most of us are mixed -- and we have more words for different combinations -- and we don't conflate race with culture (the way North Americans often do).<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> Loose thoughts - screaming into the void - hoping some other linguistically minded geeks can offer some insights or help (be it articles or personal links). <br /> <br /> I'd especially like more anecdotes or articles or references.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4831-redriotousram","name":"RedRiotousRam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4831_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-02T13:59:59-08:00","id":390314,"title":"","content":"Ah, this pertains to my interests, yes. I'm Galician. I would say I grew up with two mother languages (Castillan, most often known as Spanish, and Galician) and quite soon took a third to heart (English). I regularly <i>think</i> in all three.<br /> <br /> Because at the end of the day a language is way of thinking, the more languages you know and internalize, the more ways you have to look at the world. It is like magic, really. If you know what to look for you can guess a lot about a particular culture by understanding their language. Like the example you set with the broken glass and accidental things.<br /> <br /> Now I'm learning German and I found rather curious (or revealing!) that the words for &quot;Debts&quot; (die Schulden) and &quot;Guilt&quot; (die Schuld) are related. It helps understand the mentality of the german people (or at least the goverment) regarding the economic situation of some european countries (and their charming nickname for us P.I.G.S)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-02T21:25:25-08:00","id":390990,"title":"","content":"Actually what you're talking about steps very heavily into sociological concerns that have been growing in the past 20 years in some circles. The idea that language frames thought and indeed how you think about things isn't a new one,but the more recent cause for concern is that the modern internet will produce a homogenization of language and culture and with that decrease in different patterns of thought, decrease possible innovation and thus decrease the speed of growth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T00:57:45-08:00","id":391134,"title":"","content":"Coincidentally, my grandparents are from Galicia and Asturias - my parents then came to the Americas - now I'm in the US. And <i><u>with pride</u></i> I've had (on a few occasions) racist (Southern) Spaniards use &quot;Gallego&quot; as a slur in my direction (they had no idea of my actual ancestry - we merely spoke, instantly disagreed and disliked one another, and they ventured their guess).<br /> <br /> Which brings me to a related topic - &quot;race&quot;.<br /> <br /> In the Portuguese and Spanish colonial period, single-women were not allowed to go to the New World - so men gladly took Indigenous and African wives - resulting in mixed ancestry (regardless of whatever we look like on the surface today). I have kin of every color - and it's far more normal to identify people by country-of-origin (Cuban, Venezuelan, Peruvian, Ecuadorian, etc) than color (which is both obvious and irrelevant).<br /> <br /> It thus follows that North Americans (who conflate pigmentation with race) are bewildering (especially in that they forget all the other traits for a race - shared language, culture, religion, history). Some think all Europeans are a &quot;common people&quot; even though there are plenty who have hated and butchered each other for centuries. Sadly, those of non-European backgrounds end up drinking the Anglo-Saxon kool-aid (and thinking we're all part of the same white-washed assimilated goop).<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> <b>Anyways, I'm hoping to see more posts about language - but race &amp; culture are welcome too.</b><br /> <br /> I usually date within my own race --- which means Hispanic (of whatever color). Similarities in language, religion, culture, history, values, music, etc make it <u>much</u> easier to get along. I favor much darker skin --- but it only seems to &quot;get weird&quot; with non-Hispanics (who think British and French colonial history define all other colonies). Periodically, friends will try to introduce me to an American (who more commonly self-label as &quot;black&quot;) -- so that's one scenario where linguistic difference (I hope) can be of use. <br /> <br /> I read a study that noted that - despite initial self-selection based on appearance - language trumped appearance with people identifying their &quot;in-groups&quot;. They theorized that in a time when people lived in smaller communities, it was important to identify a neighbor or cousin (friendly) vs foreigners (possibly bandits). Language triumphs over appearance because it's much harder to replicate an accent and slang (so for example, a white Brit and black Brit are both Brits -- just as (whatevercolor) Cubans are still Cubans - and it makes sense that you'll get along with your respective nationality a lot easier).<br /> <br /> So when someone seems to confuse me with Anglo-Saxon, I ramp up the Spaglish to 11 and feign ignorance at North American &quot;racial&quot; norms (I rather be confused for a foreigner-they-don't-know ; than for a local-they-dislike). I'll also strictly refer to nationalities (so North Americans can be &quot;American&quot; - but to hell with encouraging the white/black dialogue by continuing to label people by their pigmentation and the one-drop rule).<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now I'm learning German and I found rather curious (or revealing!) that the words for &quot;Debts&quot; (die Schulden) and &quot;Guilt&quot; (die Schuld) are related. It helps understand the mentality of the german people (or at least the goverment) regarding the economic situation of some european countries (and their charming nickname for us P.I.G.S) </div> </div> </div>This is an AWESOME example.<br /> <br /> The best example I can think of (that I use to illustrate things to Anglos) is via the Greco-Roman gods - and how their names became roots for other words - very similar to Arabic.<br /> <br /> Saturday (Saturn-Day), Sunday (Sun-Day) and Monday (Moon-Day) are obvious - in Spanish, Monday is Lunes (Day of Luna).<br /> <br /> Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday are less obvious - but Marvel Comics has refreshened the old Norse gods in people's memories - Tyr's Day, Wotan/Woden/Odin's Day, Thor's Day, Freya's Day. These loosely translate to Martes (Mars Day), Miercoles (Mercury's Day), Jueves (Jove's Day) and Viernes (Venus' Day). With the Roman Empire's space in the history books and mythology (Hercules!, God of War! etc), the gods in Romance languages are easier to remember.<br /> <br /> And from there we get words. Mercury (god of travel and trade) gives us Merchant, Mercado (Market), Mercy (originally it mean &quot;alms&quot; or &quot;money&quot;, such that a homeless man begging for &quot;mercy&quot; was asking for coin) and Mercenary (in a period when people took up family jobs in the community, from father to son, and lived off barter and trade --- this new group of roaming warriors-for-hire were a new thing, hence &quot;mercenary&quot; because they traveled and fought for money). In the modern age, we're technically all &quot;mercenaries&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or we go with Mars --- whereas the Roman Ares was viewed negatively (war being destructive, long-lived warriors likely being cowards who fled, etc), the Greek Mars was viewed positively (masculinity, bravery, courage, virility). So there are obvious words like Martial Arts or Marshalls - but other words like Marido (Husband) and by extension, Marriage and Marital (because marriage granted females stability, it was the &quot;act of taking a husband&quot; or man). There are also other loose etymologies that are still consistent - I read that Mars may have originally been a god of fertility and water (impregnating the soil) and so words like Marsh, Mar (Ocean) and Marines all fit too. <br /> <br /> Anglophones refer to Boyfriends and Girlfriends (which can be confusing for non-sexual relations). Spanish differentiates between Amigos and Amigas vs Novios and Novias. And the later two can be seen almost like compounds (No-Vio &quot;didn't-see&quot;, ie, love is blind or people blinded by love). And of course, we have a few dozen other expressions for sexiness &amp; sexy relations.<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> \u200bThere's another one - I think Consonantal Shifts - where in a language, consonants often get softer or blunted the deeper they go in a sentence. Most anime lovers will be familiar with when Kami (God) becomes Gami such as in Shinigami (Death God). <br /> <br /> So &quot;Lawyer&quot; seems like a dumb modernism. The good old word was Advocate (such as &quot;The Devil's Advocate&quot;). In Spanish we'd say Abogado. And then you can line both words together and see the shifts naturally......such as A (DV/B) O (C/K/G) A (T/D) O/E (the ending changes to match the language - but everything else lines up well).<br /> <br /> .<br /> <br /> It's also great to look at when a language has words <i>that can't be translated easily</i>. When they come up with a single-word that requires a sentence to explain in another language. German Schadenfreude (the feeling of joy or pleasure when one sees another fail or suffer misfortune) is a great example. And going off the racial discussion, it's note-worthy that most other Nordic languages (Danish, Norwegian, Swedish, Finnish) have similar terms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> the modern internet will produce a homogenization of language and culture and with that decrease in different patterns of thought, decrease possible innovation and thus decrease the speed of growth </div> </div> </div>My biggest concern is this expectation of &quot;assimilation&quot; - when there is no such thing as a &quot;global culture&quot;. The &quot;norm&quot; in the US is WASP (white Anglo-Saxon Protestant) - and I'm not an Anglo-Saxon (nor do I wish to be). I also find it a bit of a tragedy that Irish, Italians and a few other races have given up their identity just to adopt the problems of a different (and previously oppressive, to them) group.<br /> <br /> Luckily (I hope), linguistic norms are also deeply tied with culture --- as a Hispanic, yes, we absorb a lot of American culture and media..... but it still gets reinterpreted to our own native culture. It would make absolutely <b>zero</b> sense (for example) to import MLK and Malcom X (we've been interbreeding for <i>centuries</i>\u200b - we never had anti-miscegenation laws); our views on sex are also fairly different (and I was <i>jubilant</i> when Chris Rock's &quot;TOP FIVE&quot; got the bit about Hispanic grandma's so spot-on); and there are associations baked into the languages themselves. <br /> <br /> Last I checked, most rankings for &quot;top languages&quot; (in terms of raw population, and none of that frou frou cultural bullshit) consistently rank English (a half dozen major countries + the language of international business), Spanish (2-3 dozen countries) and Arabic (language of religious bilingual populations) as at the top ---- with various types of Chinese and Hindi competing based off raw-population (despite that they're mostly within their own home country).<br /> <br /> It's a tragedy to see languages die out.<br /> <br /> But I'm hoping the top 3-4 will continue to be different enough from one another that people favor being multilingual rather than a mish-mash (and yes, I can use Spanglish --- but I jump to pure English or pure Spanish easily, and as a bonus, I find Arabic much easier to study thanks to commonalities with Romance languages).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4831-redriotousram","name":"RedRiotousRam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4831_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T22:06:26-08:00","id":392337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>RedRiotousRam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/390293-linguistic-psychology-bilingualism-help#post390293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some academics noticed that aborigines in Australia speak a language with a very strong sense of orientation (North / South / East / West). Whereas in English, such directions are only used for large distances - they also use them for small distances (you might say &quot;your (East/South/North/West) foot&quot; as opposed to &quot;your (Left/Right) foot&quot;. The result is that even 5 year olds could tell how everyone was oriented (no windows, no clues) - even when grown scientists could not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Monolingual Aussie here but I'm side-eyeing that claim with prejudice. <a href=\"http://www.abc.net.au/indigenous/map/\" target=\"_blank\">This </a>is a map of languages. Not dialects, <i>languages, </i>spoken by Aboriginal Australians prior to invasion. Some of those languages have survived. Aboriginal Australians do not speak a 'langauge' but a multitude, and those languages are not as related as one suspects. One does not expect the same language to be spoken in Norway as in Turkey, so it follows that given the geographic spread of Australia that there would be similar levels of difference (prior to invasion and settlement). Even if early (and sadly, some current) ethnographers and anthropologists came in with more than a little baggage about what counts as 'real' and 'worthy' of study.<br /> <br /> I mean, this is just a small example, and I'm speaking as a white person who has worked with Aboriginal Australian groups in a number of ways, but the erasure of language complexity within oppressed groups is particularly endemic to conquered peoples, and language is a very fraught subject in the Australian context. That particular example about direction gets a little bit 'mystic native' and 'totes good trackers hey' for me, when the linguistic competencies shown in Aboriginal communities is FAR more interesting (new languages are developing in several areas, following removals of children and their settlement in camps and schools where they have created a language from the remnants of the culture they remember and it's being passed down and down with their own grammar and structure and so on). Or the complexities of community and language - mother tongue is not just a figure of speech in many communities, you literally speak a different language with/about your mother-in-law. And the way family and cultural bonds form connections that do not exist in other cultures is fascinating too.<br /> <br /> *stops to breathe*<br /> <br /> Uh, so I feel super strongly about Aboriginal languages in Australia, apparently.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4822-loup","name":"Loup","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4822_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-04T11:39:31-08:00","id":392797,"title":"","content":"Short one that I'm surprised no one has brought this one up.<br /> <br /> While the trope phrase is discredited (Eskimos have 100 words for snow), the basic concept of &quot;things that are important in a culture get many words for the same basic thing&quot;.<br /> <br /> The example above about the different kinds of terms we crazy Americans have for murders and thieves. We also have lots of words for money (dollars, bucks, cash, mullah, simoleons), multiple phrases for sex (do it, sleep together, screw, fuck), and many terms for various kinds of violence (beat, nail, massacre, knock around), plus a few I'm probably forgetting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-04T12:05:34-08:00","id":392840,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.cracked.com/article_22088_9-foreign-terms-that-put-english-profanity-to-shame.html\" target=\"_blank\"> potentially relevant Cracked.com Article</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-04T12:14:43-08:00","id":392846,"title":"","content":"Here in Galicia we have circa 700 words for rain (most of them are slighty variants of more common words used only in very specific village, but still).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-04T13:45:55-08:00","id":392974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/390293-linguistic-psychology-bilingualism-help?p=392846#post392846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here in Galicia we have circa 700 words for rain (most of them are slighty variants of more common words used only in very specific village, but still).</div> </div> </div> </div>How easily can you understand Portuguese may I ask? I know some linguists say it and Galician are essentially the same language.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-05T04:10:52-08:00","id":393639,"title":"","content":"They were, and we can understand each other pretty well I guess, with some effort. Though is a bit like Italian and Spanish actually, common root, sound kinda similar-ish, but completely different languages anyway. You can hear and understand most of it, but you cannot speak it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-06T16:51:57-08:00","id":395594,"title":"","content":"Ha! Well, that's telling, isn't it? I don't know if Nippon society is today as misoginistic as it sometimes appear in media, but there you are. Couldn't tell, having absolutely zero contact with actual japanese people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}